4.2 The Floating-Point Boundary and Era Splitting
Standard 64-bit floating-point computation hits precision limits at approximately k ≈ 53. Beyond this point, εk becomes smaller than machine epsilon — computationally indistinguishable from zero though it remains structurally nonzero.
Era splitting resolves this:
Divide the k range into discrete eras (e.g., k=1→10, k=11→20, k=21→30)
At each era boundary, reset the computational clock while preserving structural state
The 3/2 reciprocal invariant acts as the verification check across era boundaries
Run multiple era configurations and select the minimum-variance solution
v1.4 reframing: era splitting is a special case of p-power scaling where p is chosen to keep k within the floating-point safe range. The closed-form orbit formula T^k(x0) = x0 / (λ^k + a(1−λ^k)x0) allows direct computation at arbitrary k without sequential stepping, making era splitting optional for pure mathematical analysis.

